###### Abstract

We perform a global 2.5D general-relativistic radiation magnetohydrodynamic simulation of supercritical accretion onto a neutron star with a 2\\times 10^{10} G dipolar magnetic field, as a model of a neutron-star-powered ultraluminous X-ray source (ULX). We compute a lower limit on the total luminosity of \\sim 2.5\\,L_{\\rm Edd}, and find the radiation to be highly beamed by the accretion disk outflows. The apparent isotropic luminosity, which is a function of the viewing angle, reaches a maximum above 100\\,L_{\\rm Edd}, consistent with the luminosities observed in ULXs.

